Steps to get it live at azamagr.github.io
1. Create the repo (name matters)
On GitHub, create a new repository named exactly azamagr.github.io (must match your username, lowercase). This special name is what makes GitHub Pages serve it at the root domain.
2. Get the project onto your machine
Download all the files above from this chat into a folder, keeping the same structure (src/ subfolder, .github/workflows/ subfolder, etc.).
3. Install dependencies
bashnpm install
4. Push to GitHub
bashgit init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial portfolio"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/azamagr/azamagr.github.io.git
git push -u origin main
5. Turn on GitHub Pages via Actions
In the repo: Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source, select GitHub Actions (not "Deploy from a branch"). The workflow I added (.github/workflows/deploy.yml) will then run automatically on this push, build the Vite project, and publish it.
6. Check it
Go to the Actions tab, watch the Deploy to GitHub Pages run finish (green check), then visit:
https://azamagr.github.io
It can take a minute or two after the first successful run to go live.
A few things worth knowing

Every future git push to main re-triggers the workflow and redeploys automatically — no manual build step needed.
vite.config.js has base: "/" because this is a username.github.io repo (root domain). If you ever host it as a normal project repo instead (e.g. github.com/azamagr/portfolio), you'd change that to base: "/portfolio/".
You still need to drop in your real media (headshot photo for About.jsx, video files for Hero.jsx) — those are referenced by path but not included, since I don't have your actual assets.
If npm install complains about anything, run npm run dev locally first to confirm it all boots before pushing — cheaper to catch issues on your machine than in CI.
